      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 The Association of University Radiologists. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "RATIONALE AND OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Ventilation heterogeneity is impossible to detect with spirometry. Alternatively, pulmonary ventilation can be imaged three-dimensionally using inhaled (129)Xe magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). To date, such images have been quantified primarily based on ventilation defects. Here, we introduce a robust means to transform (129)Xe MRI scans such that the underlying ventilation distribution and its heterogeneity can be quantified."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Quantitative (129)Xe ventilation MRI was conducted in 12 younger (24.7\u2009±\u20095.2 years) and 10 older (62.2\u2009±\u20097.2 years) healthy individuals, as well as in 9 younger (25.9\u2009±\u20096.4 yrs) and 10 older (63.2\u2009±\u20096.1 years) asthmatics. The younger healthy population was used to establish a reference ventilation distribution and thresholds for six intensity bins. These bins were used to display and quantify the ventilation defect region (VDR), the low ventilation region (LVR), and the high ventilation region (HVR)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The ventilation distribution in young subjects was roughly Gaussian with a mean and standard deviation of 0.52\u2009±\u20090.18, resulting in VDR\u2009=\u20092.1\u2009±\u20091.3%, LVR\u2009=\u200915.6\u2009±\u20095.4%, and HVR\u2009=\u200917.4\u2009±\u20093.1%. Older healthy volunteers exhibited a significantly right-skewed distribution (0.46\u2009±\u20090.20, P\u2009=\u20090.034), resulting in significantly increased VDR (7.0\u2009±\u20094.8%, P\u2009=\u20090.008) and LVR (24.5\u2009±\u200911.5%, P\u2009=\u20090.025). In the asthmatics, VDR and LVR increased in the older population, and HVR was significantly reduced (13.5\u2009±\u20094.6% vs 18.9\u2009±\u20094.5%, P\u2009=\u20090.009). Quantitative (129)Xe MRI also revealed altered ventilation heterogeneity in response to albuterol in two asthmatics with normal spirometry."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Quantitative (129)Xe MRI provides a robust and objective means to display and quantify the pulmonary ventilation distribution, even in subjects who have airway function impairment not appreciated by spirometry."
          }
        ]
      },
